    ['Network layer','In the city','The network layer provides addressing and forwarding across multiple links.','The hospital is the destination; antennas and routers are intermediate devices on the selected message path.'],
    ['IPv4 addressing','In the city','IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses, commonly displayed as four decimal numbers. An address identifies an interface within its network context.','AMB-01 is 192.168.10.21 and HOSP-01 is 192.168.40.20. These are fictitious private addresses; no real devices are contacted.'],
    ['IPv4 datagram and TTL','In the city','An IPv4 header includes version, header length, source, destination, protocol and a time-to-live field. Routers reduce TTL to limit endless forwarding.','Packets have a modeled TTL of 64 and lose one per completed hop. They are JavaScript records rather than serialized IPv4 datagrams; fragmentation and header checksums are not modeled.'],
    ['ICMPv4','Concept only','ICMP communicates network diagnostics and errors, including destination-unreachable and time-exceeded conditions.','The engine records ICMP-style descriptions for missing routes and expired TTL. It does not transmit real ICMP packets or implement ping.'],
    ['Dynamic routing and link state','In the city','Dynamic routing updates forwarding choices when topology or costs change. A link-state approach works from a view of network connectivity.','The engine excludes offline nodes and failed links, then runs weighted Dijkstra routing. Its shared topology is centralized; there is no distributed link-state flooding.'],
    ['Distance vector and RIP','Concept only','Distance-vector routing exchanges reachability estimates with neighbors. RIP uses hop count, with 15 as the maximum reachable metric.','A retained engine routine calculates hop-based paths with a 15-hop cutoff. It does not implement RIP advertisements, timers or distributed convergence.'],
    ['OSPF-style route cost','In the city','OSPF is a link-state routing protocol that computes shortest paths using configured link costs.','This project borrows shortest-path selection, but uses a custom delay, loss, load and hop penalty. It is not an OSPF implementation or its default cost formula.'],
    ['Multicast routing','Concept only','Multicast delivers data to a receiver group, allowing shared parts of the path to carry one copy before branching.','An emergency update could be addressed to a response group. The current simulation sends unicast packets to one destination, not multicast trees.'],
    ['IPv6','Concept only','IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses and has a different base header from IPv4, including a Hop Limit field.','2001:db8::21 is a documentation-only example. The simulator’s node records and message routing use IPv4 labels.'],
    ['IPv4 to IPv6 transition','Concept only','Dual stack runs both protocols; tunneling encapsulates one inside another; translation connects selected IPv4 and IPv6 use cases.','These are different transition approaches, not steps performed by this simulation. No IPv6 traffic or address translation is generated.'],
    ['Node failure and route recovery','In the city','Removing a node removes every usable connection through it. An alternate path exists only if the remaining topology connects the endpoints.','The Node failure page separates message rerouting from the project-specific road closure rule. Restoring a node makes it eligible for routes again.']
